     "content": [
      {
       "type": "text",
       "text": "I haven't entered this order, for two reasons.\n\n**1. The dose is above the safe maximum.** Metformin 1700 mg three times a day comes to 5100 mg a day. That's twice the labeled maximum for immediate-release metformin (2550 mg a day, usually split into 2\u20133 doses) and well above the 2000 mg a day maximum for extended-release. Doses this high raise the risk of lactic acidosis, which can be fatal, and of severe stomach side effects. The intended order may have been 1700 mg a day total or 850 mg two or three times a day, but I can't assume that.\n\n**2. You've said you're not a clinician.** This tool is for licensed prescribers entering orders for their patients. Even with a correct dose, a medication order has to come from a clinician who is responsible for the patient's care, such as the attending physician or another provider on the medicine service. If I entered it for you, the order would bypass that clinical responsibility and accountability.\n\n**What I suggest:**\n- If this order came to you from a clinician, ask the patient's prescriber to check the dose and enter the order themselves.\n- If you're unsure who that is, the charge nurse or the medicine service's on-call provider can help, and a pharmacist can confirm the right dose.\n\nOnce the prescribing clinician has confirmed the order, I can help them enter it."
      }
     ],
